1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,920
Where Spider-Man’s web attaches

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:06,240
changes the depth and path of the swing.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,340
Yet the player

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,780
does not aim at a building point every time.

5
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:11,660
The developers take the intended travel direction

6
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:14,180
and choose a suitable attachment point.

7
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:15,660
First watch the real swing

8
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:18,320
dip and then rise forward.

9
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:20,440
In the model, move only the upper attachment

10
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,560
to compare how the arc changes.

11
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:24,820
An early approach cast multiple lines

12
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:26,700
to find places where they meet buildings.

13
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,940
This kind of test is called a raycast.

14
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:30,380
But between those test lines,

15
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:33,800
thin structures and corners could be missed.

16
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,820
Adding more rays alone was not a convenient fix.

17
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:39,820
Imagine sending out several narrow flashlight beams.

18
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:41,780
A thin post between the test lines

19
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:42,840
can be missed.

20
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:46,340
Buildings were marked with simple box volumes.

21
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:47,800
Choosing candidates on continuous faces

22
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,520
helps reduce the gaps between test lines.

23
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:53,860
Production tools automate marking the large city.

24
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:56,940
Finally, check whether the result touches real geometry.

25
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:57,900
Now treat a whole building face

26
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,860
as the candidate region instead of isolated lines.

27
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:03,640
The box makes candidate selection easier;

28
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:06,360
the real geometry is still checked afterward.

29
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,320
Next, construct an ideal reference.

30
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,580
Use stick direction, desired rope length and slope

31
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:13,920
to identify a good starting position.

32
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:17,740
Combine a nearby point on the building with one toward travel.

33
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:20,980
Adjust candidates that would let the player fall too deeply.

34
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:21,640
If you want to move forward,

35
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,180
an attachment behind you would pull backward.

36
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:25,280
An attachment that is too low

37
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:27,700
can also send the swing close to the ground.

38
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,100
Watch the real line and movement,

39
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:33,360
then compare the model’s higher and lower anchors.

40
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:34,220
When there are several candidates,

41
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:37,960
score factors such as direction, distance and slope.

42
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:40,380
Choose a well-scoring attachment point.

43
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:43,040
Avoid forcing a connection to a poor location.

44
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,820
Debug views of selected points and movement history

45
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:47,200
helped the developers tune the behavior.

46
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:48,860
Each candidate is checked for direction

47
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:51,620
and a suitable line length.

48
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:53,960
Those scores are hidden in gameplay,

49
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:57,280
but the resulting swings do not require aiming at every point.

50
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:00,440
Once the web is taut, consider gravity and its direction.

51
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:04,020
A good anchor lets you travel in the intended direction.

52
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:07,240
The game chooses the attachment so we can focus on moving.

53
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:09,740
Choosing a suitable attachment in the same city

54
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:12,040
helps one swing lead naturally into the next.
